**assistant**:
> This is a **submodule authentication problem**, not a Python/dependency problem.
> 
> Your server is trying to fetch:
> 
> ```text
> https://github.com/jrising/chatwrap.git/
> ```
> 
> but GitHub no longer allows password auth over HTTPS, and DreamHost has no interactive way to ask for a username/token. Best fix: make the `chatwrap` submodule use **SSH**, and give the server an SSH key that can read that repo.
> 
> ## 1. Change `chatwrap` submodule URL locally
> 
> From your local `arachne` repo:
> 
> ```bash
> cd ~/projects/arachne
> 
> git config -f .gitmodules submodule.chatwrap.url git@github.com:jrising/chatwrap.git
> git submodule sync chatwrap
> 
> git add .gitmodules
> git commit -m "Use SSH URL for chatwrap submodule"
> git push origin HEAD:master
> ```
> 
> Use `main` instead of `master` if that is actually your deployment branch, but your log shows `master`.
> 
> ## 2. Add an SSH key on DreamHost for GitHub
> 
> SSH into DreamHost:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh existencia
> ```
> 
> Create a key if one does not already exist:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "dreamhost-arachne-chatwrap" -f ~/.ssh/github_chatwrap
> ```
> 
> Press enter for no passphrase, unless you have an agent setup.
> 
> Then configure SSH:
> 
> ```bash
> cat >> ~/.ssh/config <<'EOF'
> 
> Host github.com
>     HostName github.com
>     User git
>     IdentityFile ~/.ssh/github_chatwrap
>     IdentitiesOnly yes
> EOF
> 
> chmod 700 ~/.ssh
> chmod 600 ~/.ssh/config ~/.ssh/github_chatwrap
> chmod 644 ~/.ssh/github_chatwrap.pub
> ```
> 
> Now print the public key:
> 
> ```bash
> cat ~/.ssh/github_chatwrap.pub
> ```
> 
> Add that public key in GitHub under:
> 
> ```text
> jrising/chatwrap → Settings → Deploy keys → Add deploy key
> ```
> 
> Read-only is enough unless the server needs to push, which it should not.
> 
> Test from DreamHost:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh -T git@github.com
> ```
> 
> A successful GitHub response often says something like “Hi jrising/chatwrap! You've successfully authenticated…” or “Hi USERNAME! … shell access is not provided.”
> 
> ## 3. Adjust the server Git commands in your script
> 
> Because your server seems to fetch submodules during the parent fetch, make this change to avoid failing before the updated `.gitmodules` is pulled.
> 
> Replace this in the remote block:
> 
> ```bash
> git fetch origin "$branch"
> git checkout "$branch"
> git pull --ff-only origin "$branch"
> ```
> 
> with:
> 
> ```bash
> git -c fetch.recurseSubmodules=false fetch origin "$branch"
> git checkout "$branch"
> git -c fetch.recurseSubmodules=false pull --ff-only origin "$branch"
> 
> git submodule sync --recursive
> git submodule update --init --recursive
> ```
> 
> Important: **do not** use:
> 
> ```bash
> git submodule update --remote
> ```
> 
> unless you want the server to float to the newest `chatwrap` commit. For reproducible deployment, the parent `arachne` repo should pin the exact `chatwrap` commit.
> 
> ## 4. If GitHub refuses the same key elsewhere
> 
> GitHub deploy keys are normally tied to one repository. Since this key is only for `chatwrap`, that is fine.
> 
> If later you need SSH access to multiple private repos from DreamHost, you may want either:
> 
> - a GitHub machine-user account with one SSH key, or
> - separate SSH keys plus host aliases like `github.com-chatwrap`.
> 
> For now, the simple deploy-key setup above should solve this failure.
