**assistant**:
> This failure is now happening **locally**, before the server is touched:
> 
> ```text
> Command: git -C "$submodule_path" push origin "HEAD:$submodule_branch"
> ```
> 
> So the DreamHost deploy key worked, but your **local machine** cannot push to `git@github.com:jrising/chatwrap.git`.
> 
> The deploy key you added on GitHub is for the **server reading** `chatwrap`. It does not give your laptop permission to push.
> 
> ## Fix local GitHub access for `chatwrap`
> 
> On your local machine, check:
> 
> ```bash
> cd ~/projects/arachne/chatwrap
> git remote -v
> ssh -T git@github.com
> ```
> 
> If `ssh -T` says permission denied, add your **local machine’s** SSH public key to your GitHub account:
> 
> ```bash
> ls ~/.ssh/*.pub
> cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
> ```
> 
> Then add that key at:
> 
> ```text
> GitHub → Settings → SSH and GPG keys → New SSH key
> ```
> 
> Not as a deploy key on the repo — as an account key, because your local machine needs push permission.
> 
> If you do not have a local key:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "local-arachne-deploy"
> cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
> ```
> 
> Then test again:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh -T git@github.com
> git -C ~/projects/arachne/chatwrap push origin HEAD:master
> ```
> 
> Use the actual `chatwrap` branch if not `master`.
> 
> ## Improve the script: don’t push submodules unnecessarily
> 
> Right now the script pushes `chatwrap` even when it says:
> 
> ```text
> No committed file changes in submodule: chatwrap
> ```
> 
> That is over-aggressive. Replace this line:
> 
> ```bash
> git -C "$submodule_path" push origin "HEAD:$submodule_branch"
> ```
> 
> with:
> 
> ```bash
> upstream="$(git -C "$submodule_path" rev-parse --abbrev-ref --symbolic-full-name '@{u}' 2>/dev/null || true)"
> 
> if [[ -z "$upstream" ]]; then
>     echo "No upstream configured for submodule $submodule; pushing and setting upstream."
>     git -C "$submodule_path" push -u origin "HEAD:$submodule_branch"
> else
>     ahead_count="$(git -C "$submodule_path" rev-list --count "$upstream..HEAD")"
> 
>     if [[ "$ahead_count" -gt 0 ]]; then
>         echo "Submodule $submodule is $ahead_count commit(s) ahead of $upstream; pushing."
>         git -C "$submodule_path" push
>     else
>         echo "Submodule $submodule has no unpushed commits."
>     fi
> fi
> ```
> 
> ## Also improve the status label
> 
> Before the submodule loop, set:
> 
> ```bash
> CURRENT_STEP="committing and pushing submodules"
> ```
> 
> Inside the loop, add:
> 
> ```bash
> CURRENT_STEP="committing and pushing submodule: $submodule"
> ```
> 
> That way future failures won’t misleadingly say only “committing and pushing local code.”
> 
> In short: DreamHost now likely has read access; your laptop still needs write access to `chatwrap`, or the script should skip pushing when there are no unpushed submodule commits.
